<% content_for :title, "Sprint — stimulus_kanban_rails demo" %>

<header class="page">
  <h1>Sprint board</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Drag a story between columns. Every move flies through
    <code>PATCH /boards/stories/cards/:id/move</code> with an optimistic id,
    persists via <code>SprintBoard#apply_move!</code>, and broadcasts to every
    connected tab over <code>turbo_stream_from "sprint:current"</code>.
    Open this page in two browser windows side-by-side and drag in one — the
    other updates within a frame.
  </p>
  <ul class="features">
    <li><strong>Server-side board declaration</strong> — see <code>app/boards/sprint_board.rb</code></li>
    <li><strong>WIP limits</strong> on To-do (5), Doing (3), Review (2) — surfaced as a red column header when over</li>
    <li><strong>Workflow guard</strong> — a story can't enter Doing without points; try dragging an empty one and watch the move revert</li>
    <li><strong>accept_from</strong> chain — Review only accepts from Doing; Done only from Review</li>
    <li><strong>Optimistic-id reconciliation</strong> — the originator's tab suppresses its own broadcast echo (no double-render)</li>
    <li><strong>Bulk move</strong> — Cmd/Ctrl-click cards to multi-select, then drag the pile; one <code>move_bulk</code> round-trip, atomic transaction</li>
    <li><strong>Live multi-tab sync</strong> via <code>turbo_stream_from(*streamables)</code> with tenant-scoping via <code>StimulusKanbanRails.streamables_for</code></li>
  </ul>
</header>

<%# Render through the shipped board partial. It mounts the `board` +
    `board-sync` controllers, subscribes the page to the Turbo Stream,
    and lays out columns + cards from the SprintBoard declaration. %>
<%= stimulus_kanban_board(
      board: @board,
      cards: @stories,
      card_selection: "multiple") %>

<footer class="page-foot">
  <small>
    Backed by Action Cable. Open <code>/cable</code> in a second tab to see the
    WebSocket frames. View the Board class at
    <code>gem/demo/app/boards/sprint_board.rb</code> and the engine routes
    table by running <code>bin/rails routes -g cards</code>.
  </small>
</footer>
